# Changelog

All notable changes to this project will be documented in this file.

## [Unreleased]

### Added

- With `--lsp-annotations`, `--lsp-mode` prints an object with the errors and with annotations for an editor, such as the contracts that members inherit from interfaces. The protocol version reported by `--manifest` is now 3.

- A member of a module that omits its contract, with no `requires`, `ensures` or `opens`, inherits the contract that the interface the module implements declares for it.

- The postconditions of an `auto lemma` with parameters can have triggers for the parameters, written as for a quantifier, as in `ensures {f(n)} f(n) >= 0`.

- The sequences of `Library.Seq` can be used with operators, as in Viper: `s ++ t` appends, `s[i]` is the entry at index `i`, `s[i := e]` replaces it, `e in s` says whether `e` is an entry, `s[m..n]`, `s[..n]` and `s[n..]` are slices, and `s[i] := e` assigns `s[i := e]` to `s`. Sequences can be written as literals, as in `[|1, 2, 3|]` and `[||]`.

- The names `Option`, `List`, `Seq` and `Array` of the standard library are available in every program without an import, so that types such as `Seq[Int]` and `Array[Int]` can be written directly. A program's own declaration of one of these names takes precedence.

- A function can be declared `inline`, which makes its body a macro for the SMT solver instead of the subject of an axiom. The marker is ignored for a recursive function.

- Entries of arrays from `Library.Array` are accessed by indexing: `x := a[i]` reads an entry, `a[i] := v` writes it, and `a[i]` is a location for `own` and the atomic operations, as in `own(a[i], v)` and `faa(a[i], 1)`, without mentioning the cell's `value` field.

- An indexed assignment `m[k] := v` to a local map variable `m` assigns `m[k := v]` to it, also for nested maps as in `m[i][j] := v`.

- Extensions can give a meaning to core syntax that the core type checker rejects, such as indexing into something other than a map, through the hooks `claim_expr`, `claim_basic_stmt`, and `claim_location`.

- The standard library provides arrays, `Library.Array[E]`: families of heap cells addressed by index, with a predicate owning all cells of an array and a procedure allocating one.

- The standard library provides immutable sequences, `Library.Seq[E]`. Their properties are stated as lemmas in the interface `Library.SeqSpec` and proved for the implementation as lists.

- Procedures and lemmas may declare the invariants they open with an `opens` clause, e.g. `opens {}`, `opens cell(x)` or `opens inner(x, _)`. The clause replaces the mask inferred from `requires`, and callers need credit only for what it lists.

- A module can be sealed with `:>` instead of `:`, as in `module F[E: Type] :> I[E] { ... }` or `module M :> I { ... }`, and so can a single instance of a module or functor that implements an interface, as in `module V :> I = F[A]`. Outside its own body, a sealed module exposes only the members of `I`, so clients cannot depend on its representation or function definitions.

- `Library.Type` provides `default`, an unspecified value of type `T` that every implementation inherits without having to define it. A module implementing `Type` that declares its own `default` must give it type `T`.


### Changed

- Raven no longer passes facts to the SMT solver that are trivially true, such as a quantified implication whose conclusion is `true`.

- Raven gives triggers to two kinds of quantified facts it generates: that the locations of an iterated separating conjunction are distinct, and the postcondition of a `func`, which applications of the function trigger. Distinctness that holds trivially is no longer checked or assumed.

- The map comprehension `[| x: T :: e |]` is removed, as `{| x: T :: e |}` already denotes a map when `e` is not Boolean. The brackets `[| |]` now enclose sequence literals.

- The set operators `++`, `**`, `--`, `subseteq` and `choose` are now the operations of the standard library's `Library.Sets`, rather than built into the verifier. Programs using them are unaffected; `choose` may now also name a function, called qualified as in `S.choose(s)`.

- `auto pred` is now written `inline pred`, matching `inline func`, and `auto` applies only to lemmas and axioms. `auto` on a predicate or function is an error, and so is a recursive inline predicate, which previously stayed partly unexpanded.

- Every trigger of a quantifier must mention each of its variables, as for the triggers of an `auto lemma`. A trigger that does not was ignored by the SMT solver.

- Implicit parameters must come after the explicit ones. One followed by an explicit parameter was accepted, but could not be omitted in a call.

- A comprehension that combines sets element by element, such as `{| x: T :: x in s || x in t |}`, is passed to the SMT solver as an operation on whole sets rather than as a function with a quantified axiom.

- Definitions and lemmas that no proof obligation depends on, such as unused parts of the standard library, are no longer sent to the SMT solver. Small programs verify noticeably faster.

- Improved the wording and readability of the tutorial.


### Fixed

- A value of an uninstantiated functor, such as `Seq.empty`, takes its instance from the expected type, as in `s == Seq.empty` or `(Seq.empty : Seq[Int])`, like a constructor such as `List.nil`.

- Implicit functor instantiation accepts a predicate's implicit arguments when they are written, as in `Array.arr(a, m)`, and also applies to `fold` and `unfold`, as in `unfold arr(a, m)` with `arr` imported from `Library.Array`.

- Comparing a finite set with a set by `==` type-checks with the finite set on either side, not only on the right.

- A set literal takes its element type from the expected type, so that `{| {||} |}` type-checks where a `Set[Set[Int]]` is expected.

- `--extension auto` no longer takes a program that uses error credits or prophecies together with a construct every extension has, such as `match`, for a mix of extensions.

- A loop may open the invariants listed in the `opens` clause of the procedure it is in.

- Implicit functor instantiation infers a functor's arguments from a value of its rep type also when the rep type is defined as the type of a nested module, as in `rep type T = L.T`.

## [1.4.2] - 2026-10-07

### Changed

- Error messages and documentation call the parameters of a predicate or invariant after the `;` implicit parameters, rather than output parameters.

### Fixed

- Fix an unsoundness where an auto lemma could be assumed while verifying a lemma that its own proof calls, so that the two proofs justified each other.

- Folding an invariant back with its implicit arguments left out no longer lets their values change, which could be used to prove `false`. Writing an invariant's implicit arguments at `unfold` but leaving them out at `fold`, or vice versa, no longer crashes the verifier.

- A top-level declaration named like an SMT-LIB or Z3 symbol, such as a function `union`, no longer takes that symbol's place in the solver. It used to change the meaning of built-in operations such as `++`, which could make false assertions verify.

- The invariants a procedure needs are inferred from their explicit arguments only. Calls and `unfold`s no longer fail with `Invariant ... is not in the current mask` because a precondition writes an invariant's implicit arguments differently.

- The postconditions of funcs from the standard library or an included file are assumed again; they were silently dropped. Regression since 1.2.0.

- A module implementing an interface reached through `include` now inherits the interface's concrete procedures and lemmas instead of treating them as abstract. Regression since 1.2.0.

- An `own` of a fractional field declared in the standard library or an included file type-checks in a loop invariant; it was rejected as if the field were a native resource-algebra field.

- A conditional expression is right-associative, so `a ? b : c ? d : e` means `a ? b : (c ? d : e)`, as in other languages, rather than `(a ? b : c) ? d : e`.

- A loop nested in another loop no longer causes an internal error.

- A type alias that refers to itself, directly or through other aliases, is reported as an error instead of making the type checker loop.

- A trigger in the contract of a `func` no longer causes a syntax error in a body that spans several lines.

- Errors about a type or field declaration point at the declaration, instead of starting at the end of the line before it.

- An implicitly instantiated functor call returning `Bool` can be used as an assertion, as in `assert List.is_in(xs, 1)`, instead of failing with `Cannot unify type Bool with type Perm`.

- An implicitly instantiated functor call can be the first operand of an arithmetic or comparison operator, as in `List.len(xs) + 1`, instead of failing with `Cannot unify type Int with type Int or Real`.

## [1.4.1] - 2026-10-01

### Fixed

- Fix an internal error (`length mismatch in zip_exn`) in the reentrancy check when a callee requires only a prefix of an open invariant's arguments.

- Fix two unsoundnesses in the atomicity check for `atomic { ... }` blocks, which could leave a different invariant open after the block or allow a second step under an invariant closed and reopened inside it.

- The two arms of a conditional may now leave the same invariants open in a different order.

- An invariant with arguments that both arms of a conditional leave open can now be folded after the join.

- Fix an unsoundness in the reentrancy check for invariants that allowed the same instance to be opened twice if a variable was reassigned in between.

- Fix an unsoundness in the mask check at calls where credit for one invariant instance could satisfy a requirement for a whole family of instances.

## [1.4.0] - 2026-09-06

### Added

- A module or interface may implement several interfaces at once, `module M : I1, I2`, provided the parents have no common ancestor.

- The interface a module implements may be a functor application, `module M[A: I] : Base[A]`.

- Manifest fields, `field f = M.g`, which make `f` an alias for the existing field `M.g` rather than a new field.

- Physically atomic blocks, `atomic { ... }`, whose body executes as a single step. This is what lets a multi-step implementation discharge a logically atomic contract.

- A callable's leading parameters may be written as locations, `proc read(x.A.f, ...)`, naming both the `Ref` and the field.

- A functor parameterised over a field can be instantiated implicitly from the location argument of a call, e.g. `cas(x.bit, false, true)`.

- `cas`, `faa`, `xchg` and `cmpxchg` are now ordinary procedures in the standard library (`lib/library/atomics.rav`) instead of built-in statements. Use `import Library.Atomics._` or `import Library.IntAtomics._`.

- `import M._` works on an uninstantiated generic functor, with the instantiation inferred at each use.

- Callables and data constructors named with operator symbols (e.g. `+`, `<:`, `>>=`) can be declared and called infix. List cons `::` is now defined this way in the standard library, and `ListExt` is retired.

- Add `--extension auto`, which picks `default` or `eris` based on the program's syntax. Bumps `lsp_protocol_version` to 2.

### Changed

- `--strict` now also warns on every `atomic { ... }` block.
- All compilation units are type-checked before any is rewritten.
- Unbound identifiers are reported by the type checker, so their messages and locations may differ slightly.
- The abstract-invariant reach-back check now considers invariants declared by any parent of a module.
- `Library.ListM` is renamed to `Library.List`, and `List` is no longer a keyword.
- A callable's mask is computed from its own `requires` clause only, no longer from the calls in its body. Mask availability at a call site falls back to an SMT check when no syntactic match is found.

### Fixed

- Fix a crash when a generic-functor value such as `Option.some(1)` is used without being bound to a variable.
- Fix instantiation inference for a nullary constructor used as an argument, e.g. `1 :: nil`.
- Fix an `unfold`/`fold` witness clause binding the wrong variable when the predicate body has several bound variables of the same name.
- An existential in the fraction position of `own` now gets a witness when nothing else determines one.
- Fix a module being unable to inherit from another module in an included or library file.
- Fix "unknown constant" errors from Z3 for symbols reached through a manifest field.
- Fix the prophecy extension guessing the name of a generated type module.
- Fix a diagnostic being reported twice for an obligation inherited from an interface.
- Fix calls through a manifest field when the callee has an implicit ghost parameter constrained by that field.
- Fix procedures imported from an uninstantiated generic functor not being callable unqualified.
- Fix implicit instantiation rejecting calls that omit trailing implicit ghost arguments.
- Fix inconsistent naming of instantiations whose element type nests another `Proph[T]`.
- Fix the mask check for calls that omit a trailing implicit ghost argument.
- Fix nested invariants dropping a mask widening, which made recursive traversals of linked structures fail to verify.

## [1.3.0] - 2026-08-06

### Added

- Extension API: a `stmt_ext_atomicity` hook declaring how many atomic steps an extension statement takes. See `docs/ext/README.md`.
- Add `Library.Auth.full`, holding both the authoritative element and the matching fragment.
- Add a `--manifest` flag printing the version, LSP protocol version and minimum Z3 version as JSON. Releases now also ship `manifest.json` and `SHA256SUMS`.
- Substantially rework the tutorial, with cram tests for every code sample and automatically numbered cross-references.
- Add `FinSet[T]`, a built-in subtype of `Set[T]` for finite sets, and the `choose` operator for picking an element of a set. `decreases` clauses can use `FinSet`-typed measures.

### Changed

- `Library.SetRA`'s constructor, destructor and top case are renamed to `set`, `value` and `top`, matching `Library.DisjSet`.
- The "Unclosed AU token or invariant" error now names what was left open and is reported at the closing brace.
- Identifiers in error messages are printed as written in the source, without disambiguation suffixes.
- Rework the prophecy extension's `Proph` type and syntax. Resolving a one-shot prophecy twice is now a verification error.
- SMT declarations are scoped by module, so each proof obligation only sees the symbols it needs.
- Predicates and invariants without out-arguments use simpler resource algebras, which speeds up proofs with iterated separating conjunctions.
- An iterated separating conjunction's inverse function and injectivity axiom are generated once per occurrence and reused.


### Fixed

- Fix the atomicity analysis ignoring extension statements, which allowed several `faa`/`cas` steps under one open invariant.
- Fix lexing of files with CRLF line endings.
- Fix diagnostics for statements ending at an inferred semicolon extending into the next line.
- Fix the reported location of statements starting with an optional `ghost` modifier.
- A conditional under an open invariant is accepted when each arm takes at most one atomic step.
- Fix misleading errors for a functor used as a type argument that does not satisfy the required interface, and for unknown identifiers inside a functor application.
- Fix two automatic semicolon insertion issues: a bare `return` at the end of a line, and `,` directly after an operator character.
- Reject ghost allocations that initialize non-ghost fields.
- Fix a Z3 parse error on constructor names containing `'`.
- Fix a type alias and its expansion producing two incompatible instantiations of a generic functor.
- Fix witness computation for resource algebra values built with constructors such as `SetRA.set`, and support `Auth.full`.
- Give a specific error when a one-shot prophecy's resource is unavailable at `Proph.resolve`.


## [1.2.0] - 2026-08-01

### Added

- Semicolons after statements are now optional.
- Functors whose parameters are all constrained by rep-typed interfaces can be used without explicit instantiation, e.g. `M[Int]` as a type or `M.foo(3)` as a call.
- `module M = F[Int]` accepts a bare type argument for such parameters.
- Type annotations on subexpressions, `(e: T)`.
- A `decreases` clause for checking termination of recursive funcs, procs, lemmas and loops, including mutual recursion, with user-definable well-founded orders.
- Extension API: a `contract_ext` extension point for new contract clauses. See `docs/ext/README.md`.
- Several distinct instances of the same invariant can be open at once, e.g. for hand-over-hand traversal.
- `assert e with { ... }` supports `exists` goals.
- Add `--strict`, which warns about recursive funcs/lemmas without `decreases` and about uses of `free`.
- Add `--dump-library` and `--print-library-source` for viewing the library sources embedded in the binary.
- `--lsp-mode` marks locations inside library sources and gives their file path when a matching checkout is available.
- Add a tutorial, published at https://nyu-acsys.github.io/raven/tutorial/.
- Add a project landing page at https://nyu-acsys.github.io/raven/.
- `is` constructor tests and `match` expressions for `data` types.
- Extension API: a `disambiguate_expr_ext` hook for extension constructs that bind variables. See `docs/ext/README.md`.

### Changed

- Semicolons are no longer accepted between top-level module members.
- Replace 'admitted' keyword with 'free' and unify handling of admitted and free symbols in AST.
- Set prophecy extension to be the default extension.
- The active extension is passed through the pipeline explicitly instead of via global mutable state.
- Error messages throughout the verifier are clearer and more consistent.
- `assert e with { ... }` is now implemented as an extension.

### Fixed

- `--lsp-mode` keeps the file, position and message of related locations in included files.
- Fix modules implementing an interface from the standard library or an included file skipping the check for undefined members and interface axioms.
- Fix a variable named after a destructor field shadowing that destructor.
- Fix bug preventing a functor to abstract over module members of its interface.
- Fixed some loop holes when type-checking modules against interfaces.
- Fix duplicated error message when a call precondition (or a desugared loop invariant) fails to verify.
- Fix rewriting of List[T] types inside abstract modules (#38).
- Reject procedure/lemma calls embedded in expressions (e.g. return statements) instead of failing later with a confusing error.
- Fix contracts of recursive (and mutually-recursive) functions failing to verify by auto-generating an inductive proof lemma for each function's ensures clause.
- Fix tuple constructors in the SMT-LIB encoding being rejected as ambiguous by newer Z3 versions.
- Give a clear error for `import M._` on an uninstantiated generic functor (#40).
- Explain type mismatches between two names for the same underlying module.
- Fix a backend crash for tuples wider than 10 components (#28).
- Report which `--extension` to select when a file uses syntax from an inactive extension.
- Fix a spurious "Unknown identifier" error for atomic procedures inherited unchanged from an interface.
- Reject return variables in a callable's own `requires` clause.
- Reject direct writes to non-ghost fields from ghost code.
- Fix a crash when inferring a type argument from an ambiguous argument such as `{||}`.
- Fix an unsoundness where reassigning a variable between `unfold` and `fold` let resources escape an invariant.
- Fix `create()`-style procedures being rejected at call sites that do not mention the invariant (#23).
- Fix a spurious "Cannot assign to value" error on `val x := new(...)`.
- Fix an unsoundness in `assert e with { ... }` that allowed proving impure facts for free.

## [1.1.0] - 2026-01-27

### Added

- Added ExtensionAPI as a way to modularly add and remove front-end features in Raven.
- Added cmpxchg atomic operation.
- Added a new cmdline option `--extension` which (presently) takes one of three values: `default`, `eris`, and `prophecy`. This option can be used to select which extension of Raven are active for a given run.

### Changed

- Upgraded [Array interface](test/arrays/array.rav) from an interface to a module.
- Finished implementation of [rdcss](test/ext_prophecy/rdcss.rav).
- Added expr_ext_rewrite_types, stmt_ext_rewrite_types to ExtAPI.
- Added parser support for auCommit resources; enable 'helping'.
- Expanded definition of "word-sized" types to more flexible types such as algebraic sums of base types.
- Overhauled extensions to follow namespace-style convention.
- Added support for helping by allowing auPred resources to refer to another proc; overhauling parser, type-checking and rewriting.
- Updated syntax so that commitAU always requires at least two argument: token and a tuple of return values (if function returns nothing then it should be ()); and an optional third argument which is a tuple of the callable args (which goes as the middle argument). This optional argument becomes required if the token refers to a different procedure.


### Fixed

- Fixed bug in ComputeStats functionality with looking up abstract members without entering modules.
- Fixed bug regarding missing quantifiers for auto lemmas with arguments.
- Fixed two bugs in type checker related to interface aliasing (#35).
- Fixed a bug causing a crash on datatype destructors.
- Sanitizing JSON output in LSP mode.
- Catch module-level var declarations in type checker rather than parser (#31).
- Fix type-checking bug that allowed access to members of interfaces from outside of their scopes.

## [1.0.1] - 2025-11-22

### Fixed

- Supressed generating log files unless debug mode is used.

- Fixed multiple redundant local variable declarations due to excessive havocing.
- Variables declared inside while loops are now declared as local variables when while loops are rewritten to proc calls. (#29).
- Fix missing locations in certain expressions being constructed.
- Fix SMT Backend crash with "_" ident (#22).
- Fix crash on FPU stmt in while loops (#27).
- Added if_block before making call to while_loop proc.
- Fix spurious type error when type-checking inherited modules (#25).
- Fix spurious type error when assigning from data type destructor (#26).

## [1.0.0] - 2025-06-02

### Added

- Initial release of Raven.
